This keeps the implementation short by putting both arrays into one list and sorting it. The fact that the inputs are already sorted is not used here, so this is easier to write but slower than a merge or binary search solution.

The even-length case needs a `double` cast before adding the two middle values. Otherwise, integer division could remove the decimal part.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;

class Solution {
    public double median(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        ArrayList<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int value : arr1) {
            values.add(value);
        }
        for (int value : arr2) {
            values.add(value);
        }

        Collections.sort(values);

        int size = values.size();
        if (size % 2 == 1) {
            return values.get(size / 2);
        }

        return ((double) values.get(size / 2 - 1) +
                values.get(size / 2)) / 2.0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O((m + n) log(m + n))` because of sorting.

Space complexity: `O(m + n)` for the combined list. Neither input array is modified.

Instead of building the whole combined array, this version finds the element at a requested position. It removes roughly half of the remaining candidates at each recursive call.

The median is the middle element for an odd total length. For an even length, the helper is called twice for the two middle positions.

### Dry run
With `[1, 3]` and `[2, 4]`, the two target positions are `1` and `2` using zero-based indexing. The helper returns `2` and `3`, giving `(2 + 3) / 2.0`.

```java
class Solution {
    public double median(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        int total = arr1.length + arr2.length;

        if (total % 2 == 1) {
            return findKth(arr1, 0, arr2, 0, total / 2);
        }

        int left = findKth(arr1, 0, arr2, 0, total / 2 - 1);
        int right = findKth(arr1, 0, arr2, 0, total / 2);
        return ((double) left + right) / 2.0;
    }

    private int findKth(int[] arr1, int start1, int[] arr2, int start2, int k) {
        if (start1 >= arr1.length) {
            return arr2[start2 + k];
        }
        if (start2 >= arr2.length) {
            return arr1[start1 + k];
        }
        if (k == 0) {
            return Math.min(arr1[start1], arr2[start2]);
        }

        int step = (k - 1) / 2;
        int index1 = Math.min(start1 + step, arr1.length - 1);
        int index2 = Math.min(start2 + step, arr2.length - 1);

        if (arr1[index1] <= arr2[index2]) {
            int removed = index1 - start1 + 1;
            return findKth(arr1, index1 + 1, arr2, start2, k - removed);
        }

        int removed = index2 - start2 + 1;
        return findKth(arr1, start1, arr2, index2 + 1, k - removed);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log(m + n))` for each kth search, so the even case still stays logarithmic.

Space complexity: `O(log(m + n))` from recursion. The input arrays are unchanged.

Only the values around the middle are needed, so there is no need to store every merged value. Two pointers walk through the arrays, and `previous` and `current` keep the last two values seen.

The loop stops after reaching the middle position. This is still linear time, but it uses constant extra space and is simpler than a partition search.

### Edge cases
The loop also works when one array is empty because a missing side contributes `INT_MAX`. The problem must still contain at least one value overall.

```cpp
#include <climits>

class Solution {
public:
    double median(vector<int> &arr1, vector<int> &arr2) {
        int total = static_cast<int>(arr1.size() + arr2.size());
        int needed = total / 2;
        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;
        int previous = 0;
        int current = 0;

        for (int step = 0; step <= needed; step++) {
            previous = current;

            int value1 = i < static_cast<int>(arr1.size()) ? arr1[i] : INT_MAX;
            int value2 = j < static_cast<int>(arr2.size()) ? arr2[j] : INT_MAX;

            if (value1 <= value2) {
                current = value1;
                i++;
            } else {
                current = value2;
                j++;
            }
        }

        if (total % 2 == 1) {
            return current;
        }

        return (static_cast<double>(previous) + current) / 2.0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O((m + n) / 2)`, which is `O(m + n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input arrays are not modified.

The two arrays are already sorted, so a normal merge is enough. I copy the merged values into one vector, then read the middle value, or the average of the two middle values when the size is even.

### Dry run
For `[1, 3]` and `[2, 4]`, the merged array is `[1, 2, 3, 4]`. The middle values are `2` and `3`, so the answer is `2.5`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    double median(vector<int> &arr1, vector<int> &arr2) {
        vector<int> merged;
        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;

        while (i < static_cast<int>(arr1.size()) &&
               j < static_cast<int>(arr2.size())) {
            if (arr1[i] <= arr2[j]) {
                merged.push_back(arr1[i]);
                i++;
            } else {
                merged.push_back(arr2[j]);
                j++;
            }
        }

        while (i < static_cast<int>(arr1.size())) {
            merged.push_back(arr1[i]);
            i++;
        }

        while (j < static_cast<int>(arr2.size())) {
            merged.push_back(arr2[j]);
            j++;
        }

        int total = static_cast<int>(merged.size());
        if (total % 2 == 1) {
            return merged[total / 2];
        }

        return (static_cast<double>(merged[total / 2 - 1]) +
                merged[total / 2]) / 2.0;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(m + n)`.

Space complexity: `O(m + n)` for the merged vector. The input arrays are not changed.

## Intuition
Both arrays are already sorted, so instead of sorting them again, we can merge them in sorted order using two pointers.

- i points to arr1
- j points to arr2
- Compare arr1[i] and arr2[j]
- Put the smaller element into the new array arrN
- Continue until both arrays are completely merged

Once the merged sorted array is obtained:

If the total length is odd, the median is the middle element.
If the total length is even, the median is the average of the two middle elements.

## Approach
1. Create a new array of size arr1.length + arr2.length.
2. Use two pointers i and j to traverse the two sorted arrays.
3. Compare the current elements and insert the smaller one into arrN.
4. Copy the remaining elements from whichever array is not exhausted.
5. Calculate the median using the middle index/indices.
6. Return the median.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public double median(int[] arr1, int[] arr2) {
        int i=0;
        int j=0;
        int k=0;
        int[] arrN = new int[arr1.length+arr2.length];
        while(i<arr1.length && j<arr2.length){
            if(arr1[i]<arr2[j]){
                arrN[k]=arr1[i];
                i++;
                k++;
            }
            else{
                arrN[k]=arr2[j];
                j++;
                k++;
            }
        }
            while(i<arr1.length){
            arrN[k]=arr1[i];
            i++;
            k++;
        }
            while(j<arr2.length){
            arrN[k]=arr2[j];
            j++;
            k++;
        }

        double ans=0;
        int n=arr1.length + arr2.length;
        if((arr1.length+arr2.length)%2==0){
            ans = ((double) arrN[n/2] + (double) arrN[n/2 -1])/2.0;
        }
        else{
            ans = (double)arrN[n/2];
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(n + m)

Where:

n = arr1.length
m = arr2.length

Each element from both arrays is visited once.
**Space Complexity:**
O(n + m)

The new array arrN stores all elements of both arrays.

